WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.891
Go's type system supports defensive design
when teams use it deliberately.

2
00:00:03.971 --> 00:00:06.355
Concrete types can make important meanings
visible.

3
00:00:06.435 --> 00:00:08.449
Typed constants can limit supported
states.

4
00:00:08.529 --> 00:00:11.655
Explicit conversions can force a developer
to acknowledge a change in

5
00:00:11.735 --> 00:00:12.529
representation.

6
00:00:12.609 --> 00:00:16.424
Small interfaces can describe the exact
behavior a caller needs rather than granting

7
00:00:16.504 --> 00:00:17.988
broad access to an entire object.

8
00:00:18.068 --> 00:00:20.982
Broad interfaces and ambiguous data
structures deserve review.

9
00:00:21.062 --> 00:00:24.453
A value of type interface{} or any may be
necessary near generic boundaries,

10
00:00:24.533 --> 00:00:27.606
but it should not become a way to avoid
thinking about shape, meaning,

11
00:00:27.686 --> 00:00:28.375
and ownership.

12
00:00:28.455 --> 00:00:31.951
Type assertions should be checked and
close to the boundary where uncertainty

13
00:00:32.031 --> 00:00:32.402
exists.

14
00:00:32.482 --> 00:00:36.191
Once data is validated, the rest of the
application should work with clearer types.

15
00:00:36.271 --> 00:00:38.814
Error returns are also security-relevant
control flow.

16
00:00:38.894 --> 00:00:41.861
An ignored error can turn a failed parse,
failed permission check,

17
00:00:41.941 --> 00:00:45.756
failed write, failed cleanup, or failed
network operation into misleading success.

18
00:00:45.836 --> 00:00:47.903
The secure habit is not to panic on every
error.

19
00:00:47.983 --> 00:00:50.738
It is to decide what each failure means,
preserve invariants,

20
00:00:50.818 --> 00:00:54.739
return safe responses, and log useful
information without exposing sensitive values.

21
00:00:54.819 --> 00:00:57.786
Zero values are one of Go's strengths, but
they still need context.

22
00:00:57.866 --> 00:01:00.303
A zero timeout, empty allowlist, nil map,
empty token,

23
00:01:00.383 --> 00:01:03.880
or missing configuration value may be safe
in one package and risky in another.

24
00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:07.245
Defaults should be documented and tested
so ordinary use is predictable.

25
00:01:07.325 --> 00:01:09.285
Safer defaults reduce maintenance burden.

26
00:01:09.365 --> 00:01:11.855
Constructors, option structs, validation
functions,

27
00:01:11.935 --> 00:01:14.690
and package APIs should make secure use
easier than risky use.

28
00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:16.678
The less special knowledge a caller needs,

29
00:01:16.758 --> 00:01:19.460
the more reliable the code becomes under
deadline pressure.
